//! # Why each case names its Rust type
//!
//! One test sampling a case index and looking the type up dynamically could not
//! exist — Rust has no runtime type registry — so the mapping from definition to
//! type is written out. That is the right shape: writing it out is what makes
//! this a compile-checked assertion that the definition `CallToolResult` really
//! does produce `wire::v2024_11_05::CallToolResult`.
//!
//! # Two tables, two jobs
//!
//! The suite has both a hand-written table and a generated one, and they are
//! not the same table with the generated one as a convenience.
//!
//! - **The hand-written table** names the Rust type for a curated set of
//!   definitions, by hand. That is what makes the *mapping* a compile-checked
//!   fact: if the generator renamed a type, this table stops compiling.
//! - **The generated table** names one entry per `(revision, definition)` pair
//!   the vendored schemas define — every one of them, 708 pairs — and is
//!   written by `build.rs` from the same schemas the values come from. That is
//!   what makes the *values* exhaustively covered: a definition nobody thought
//!   to write down is still round-tripped.
//!
//! The generated table cannot check the mapping, because it derives the type
//! path from the same naming rule the generator uses. A rule that mapped
//! `CallToolResult` onto `CallToolRequest` would produce a table that passes.
//! The hand-written table is the half that catches that, and the generated
//! table is the half that covers everything.
//!
//! The generated table is also checked against the schemas: a test asserts
//! that the pairs it names are exactly the pairs the vendored schemas define.
//! Without that, a table that silently lost a definition would leave a hole in
//! the coverage that nothing reports.

